    "what will be the result of such a program… well, the first statement, x=3; will set x to 3, then the next statement x=5; will (maybe counter-intuitively to those who know math) set x to 5, then the final statement x=x+1; we need to break down into its parts. we have the x on the left called an “lvalue” which is the same as the first x=3;, where the x is what stores the value, then we have x+1, the value of x at this point of the program is 5 because it comes after x=5; so when we do x+1 it is the same as doing 5+1, and then finally we assign that to the lvalue, so at the end of the program, x will contain 6 which is 5+1. *(insert m-programmer program that steps on this program step by step) other kinds of statements we will look at later in the book are things like function calls, return statements, conditional statements and boolean statements. I would like to touch on boolean statements before we go on. boolean values. a boolean value is a value that can either contain a true or false value. a boolean statement is something that evaluates(is calculated) to either a true or false value for example x=5>3; the x is our usual lvalue which will store the result of the following operation. the operation is 5>3 , and since 5 is more than 3, this will evaluate to true. in the C programming language and many other languages any value that is not 0 is true, and true is usually 1, so 5>3 will become the value 1 which will make x equal to 1 which means x is true now. if we did this instead, x=3>5 then the x would end up being 0 in C. other languages like Javascript have actual boolean values which are not necessarily a number at all, they just contain true or false, so the variables worn contain a 1 or a 0, instead of that they will actually contain true or false.": [
      "what will be the result of such a program… well, the first statement, x=3; will set x to 3, then the next statement x=5; will (maybe counter-intuitively to those who know math) set x to 5, then the final statement x=x+1; we need to break down into its parts. we have the x on the left called an “lvalue” which is the same as the first x=3;, where the x is what stores the value, then we have x+1, the value of x at this point of the program is 5 because it comes after x=5; so when we do x+1 it is the same as doing 5+1, and then finally we assign that to the lvalue, so at the end of the program, x will contain 6 which is 5+1. *(insert m-programmer program that steps on this program step by step) other kinds of statements we will look at later in the book are things like function calls, return statements, conditional statements and boolean statements. I would like to touch on boolean statements before we go on. boolean values. a boolean value is a value that can either contain a true or false value. a boolean statement is something that evaluates(is calculated) to either a true or false value for example x=5>3; the x is our usual lvalue which will store the result of the following operation. the operation is 5>3 , and since 5 is more than 3, this will evaluate to true. in the C programming language and many other languages any value that is not 0 is true, and true is usually 1, so 5>3 will become the value 1 which will make x equal to 1 which means x is true now. if we did this instead, x=3>5 then the x would end up being 0 in C. other languages like Javascript have actual boolean values which are not necessarily a number at all, they just contain true or false, so the variables worn contain a 1 or a 0, instead of that they will actually contain true or false.",
      "x=5 となる. x=x+1 の最終式は x=x+1 で割り切れる必要があります. 左側にある x は  ⁇ lvalue ⁇  と呼ばれるものです. これは最初の x=3 と同じです. ここで x がその値を保存するものです. 函数呼び出し,返済式,条件式, boolean式などです. ボーリアン値とは,真実または虚偽の値を含むことができる値である.ボーリアン式は,評価する物である.例えば x=5>3;xは,次の操作の結果を保存する通常のl値である.この操作は 5>3 ,および5が3より大きいので,これは真実に評価されます.Cプログラミング言語および他の多くの言語では,0でない任意の値が本当であり,通常は trueは1,したがって,5>3は1となる値となり,x=1になるため,xが真実であることを意味します.代わりに,x=3>5すると,xはCで0になります.Javascriptのような他の言語には実際のボーリアン値があり,必ずしも真実または虚偽数を含まない. 変数が実際に true または false を含む代わりに 1 または 0 を含みます",
      "lo que será el resultado de tal programa... bueno, la primera declaración, x=3; establecerá x a 3, luego la siguiente declaración x=5; voluntad (quizás contra-intuitivamente a aquellos que conocen matemáticas) set x a 5, entonces la declaración final x=x+1; tenemos que romper en sus partes. tenemos el x en la izquierda llamado un “valor” que es el mismo que el primer x=3;, donde el x es lo que almacena el valor, entonces tenemos x+1, el valor de x en este punto del programa es 5 porque viene después x=5; así que cuando hacemos x+1 es el mismo que hacer 5+1, y luego finalmente asignamos eso al valor, por lo que al final de 6 que es x + contendrá *(inserte m-programmer program that steps on this program step by step) other kinds of statements we will look at later in the book are things like function calls, return statements, conditional statements and boolean statements. Me gustaría tocar las declaraciones booleanas antes de continuar. valores booleanos. un valor booleano es un valor que puede contener un valor verdadero o falso. una declaración booleana es algo que evalúa(se calcula) a un valor verdadero o falso por ejemplo x=5 monedas3; el x es nuestro valor habitual que almacenará el resultado de la siguiente operación. la operación es 5 título3 , y ya que 5 es más de 3, esto evaluará a verdad. en el lenguaje de programación C y muchos otros idiomas cualquier valor que no sea 0 es cierto, y verdadero es generalmente 1, por lo que 5 título3 se convertirá en el valor 1 que hará x igual a 1 que significa x es verdad ahora. si lo hiciéramos en su lugar, x=3 monedas5 entonces el x terminaría siendo 0 en C. otros lenguajes como Javascript tienen valores booleanos reales que no son necesariamente un número en absoluto, sólo contienen verdadero o falso, por lo que las variables usadas contienen 1 o 0, en lugar de que realmente contendrán verdadero o falso."
    ],
